WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.159 --> 00:00:06.009
So the bigger the vision you have, the more
important it is for you on what type of a

00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:07.609
team you're going to put around you.

00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:08.609
Simple as that.

00:00:08.609 --> 00:00:12.980
And I think one of the challenges with people
when it's about putting a team around them,

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.160
they want to be something they're not.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.230
So it's everybody wants to be the Shaq or
the LeBron or the Kobe instead of realizing

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:20.230
what your strength is.

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:21.472
I'm going to talk about that a little bit
today.

00:00:21.472 --> 00:00:24.990
I'm going to talk about 10 different roles,
strengths and departments within a company.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.780
So as you're looking at this, everybody that's
looking at this, you may be at a different

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:29.920
position in your life.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:34.170
You may be a salesperson, you may be an entrepreneur
that's just getting started, maybe a mid-size

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.570
business, that you may look at this and say
that this is where we lack or maybe somebody

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:41.879
that's an executive and an owner part of a
company that's saying this is what area I

00:00:41.879 --> 00:00:44.770
am very good at and I need to reposition myself
here.

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.011
Regardless of where you're at, this is how
I want you to look at this here.

00:00:47.011 --> 00:00:48.011
This video is for you.

00:00:48.011 --> 00:00:49.140
No one else.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:50.719
I want you to look at this video as you.

00:00:50.719 --> 00:00:54.579
And as I go through this, I need you to ask
yourself, which one is me.

00:00:54.579 --> 00:00:56.690
Give me your one, two, three, yourself.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:59.200
I'm one this, two this, three this.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.040
And, if you think that's you, verify it with
somebody who knows you very well for them

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.990
to say I really think you're more this, this,
this - if you're willing to get some honest

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:09.110
feedback from somebody.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:10.429
So let's get right into it.

00:01:10.429 --> 00:01:14.550
Look, 10 different roles within a company.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.459
First, we have the biz dev.

00:01:16.459 --> 00:01:19.729
And nothing is any specific order, I'm just
telling you what's what.

00:01:19.729 --> 00:01:23.000
First you have the biz dev, someone who's
doing business development for the company.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.000
What is biz dev?

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.390
Here's what biz dev is.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:31.630
In the world of politics, if you're running
a country, an ambassador that is a U.S. ambassador

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:35.220
that deals with relations in the Middle East
-- hypothetically.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.899
The biz dev person is doing the dirty work.

00:01:37.899 --> 00:01:39.000
Very, very important role.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.060
But they do the dirty work.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:45.940
So you need to go out there and sit and talk
to the political powers, the senators, the

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:51.000
congressmen, the president of a country in
the Middle East - Iran, Kuwait, whatever it

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.000
is, and ask them.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.500
They're going to say, "we really don't like
your president, that he announced this, this,

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.710
this," or "We really don't like how this partnership
or relationship's going.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.250
We don't like how you're doing this."

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:02.270
That's biz dev.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:07.020
And the ambassador is supposed to create a
synergy of them still wanting to do business

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:08.020
together.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:13.200
And bringing the relationship that possibly
was sour, rekindle, this is strengthen, this

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.330
is rebuild, this is a lot of re, re, re -- that's
biz dev, okay?

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.040
Next, we can go into the visionary.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:25.200
The visionary is what a lot of people want
to become visionaries but not everybody is

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.200
a visionary.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.740
Let me explain to you what I mean by not everybody
is a visionary.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:33.590
If you ask in basketball, everybody wants
to be like Mike.

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:34.740
Modern day.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.340
Everybody wanted to be a Kobe.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:38.830
Or everybody wants to be a LeBron James.

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:43.650
But maybe you're a Steve Kerr, you know?

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.100
Maybe you're somebody that's a, you know,
Steph Curry.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.700
Maybe you're somebody that's just a role player.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.040
Maybe you're a sixth man.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.540
Maybe you're a big guy that you don't have
good footwork, but you're somebody that the

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:59.490
other centers get good on working with you
because you're stronger, you're pushing them

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:01.490
and bullying them, you're strengthening them.

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:05.710
Maybe you're somebody that's good at playing
with the guys and doing their legs and taping

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:06.710
and all this other stuff.

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:08.300
So what is the role you're playing?

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:11.390
A visionary would generally be called the
CEO.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:18.000
Now sometimes, some companies make the mistake
of just because somebody's title is CEO, it

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.650
doesn't mean they're a visionary.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:25.940
Sometimes companies make a mistake and they
hire a operations person who becomes a CEO

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:31.530
and what this guy who's an operations internally
but is the CEO, all he's trying to do is make

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.120
all the systems perfect.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.210
And they're wondering how come the company's
not exploding, because you're not a CEO.

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:38.530
You're operations.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:41.470
You're a COO., not a CEO.

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:42.470
You're not a face.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:45.540
You're not someone that's going to say, "We're
going to go and take over this market."

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:50.819
Let me give you a good example of a CEO.

00:03:50.819 --> 00:03:53.590
I'm going to butcher his name.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:57.599
Roberto Goizueta - This guy is a fascinating
guy to study.

00:03:57.599 --> 00:04:04.120
He's a Cuban immigrant, went to Yale, just
so you know this, he used to make $500 a month

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:10.190
as a chemical engineer, then he comes to America
and somehow, some way, after being an immigrant

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:16.259
from Cuba, he becomes the CEO of Coca Cola
and he goes from making $500 a month to getting

00:04:16.259 --> 00:04:20.650
an $83 million bonus check when he worked
at Coca Cola.

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:21.650
So why is this?

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:22.650
Why did this happen?

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:28.710
Well, for the longest time, for the longest
time, Coca Cola dominated the marketplace.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:30.520
For the longest time.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:38.940
Now, in the early 80s, Pepsi started coming
close to the point where Pepsi got 23% of

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:42.080
the market share and Coca Cola only had 22%.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:47.210
Now, Roberto gets hired in 1981 I believe.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:53.471
And his number I just gave you, 23% to 22%,
Pepsi over Coke, Pepsi moved past Coke, first

00:04:53.471 --> 00:04:54.660
time ever.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:55.660
First time ever.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:57.600
They passed Coke, Roberto comes in.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:58.910
And here's what he asked.

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:01.050
Look at the way he thinks.

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:04.880
He says, Well, I want to know this.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.400
The average human being, how much do they
drink a day?

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.900
And they said, Roberto, this is how much we're
selling right now, bottles of Coke.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:13.020
He said, no, no.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:17.440
I'm asking you how much do they consume of
drinks per day.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.460
What are you talking about?

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:28.710
Listen, how much does a person drink - water,
milk, tea, juice, ice-tea, Coke -- I want

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:29.710
to know everything.

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:30.710
How much does a person drink?

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:33.250
They're like, Oh my God, where is this guy
going with this thing here?

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:34.250
Here's how they drink.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:35.930
Then he said, that's exactly what we need
to do.

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:40.760
I want a bigger market share of how much somebody
consumes of drinks.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:41.880
Fascinating.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.510
Look what happens then.

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:45.020
So what does he come out with?

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:46.020
Here's what he comes out with.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:49.810
Most of us only know about Coca Cola, Diet
Coke, Coke Zero, etc.

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:50.810
Ready?

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:57.970
Minute Maid is a Coke product, Fanta is a
Coke product, Dasani water is a Coke product,

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:03.490
Nestea is a Coke product, Bicardi Mixers is
a Coke product.

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:04.490
Okay?

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:10.880
Flavored milks, root beer, bottled juices,
Zico coconut water -- you know how many people

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:15.800
like to drink coconut water - Powerade, his,
Simply Orange his, Hi-C, his.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:18.229
You know what ends up happening with Coca
Cola?

00:06:18.229 --> 00:06:24.919
Coca Cola goes from the top of numbers that
we're putting up which in 1981 was 4.3 billion

00:06:24.919 --> 00:06:32.080
dollars per year to 152 billion dollars per
year.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:38.389
They're today selling two billion Coca Cola
bottles per day, two billion is what they

00:06:38.389 --> 00:06:40.440
sell per day.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.360
Now, why did that happen?

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:47.850
It happened because Coca Cola went and hired
a visionary.

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:50.810
Not because they got an operations guy - they
hired a visionary.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:54.229
And the visionary said, we need to get more
of this.

00:06:54.229 --> 00:06:56.560
So are you a CEO type yourself?

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:01.389
That could motivate you, but maybe you're
not the visionary type, and that's fine.

00:07:01.389 --> 00:07:03.259
Operations, what's operations?

00:07:03.259 --> 00:07:07.949
Systems, CRM, flow, how long does it take
from the person that looks at your website

00:07:07.949 --> 00:07:12.620
to buying the product to converting to being
a be back customer, what can we make to get

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:14.120
these customers that we have.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:18.229
We have 60,000 customers here, what can we
do to get the 60,000 customers to help us

00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:22.651
get to their 60,000 customers because we're
so good on the front end, we're not doing

00:07:22.651 --> 00:07:24.040
good on this side here.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:25.699
What CRM do we need?

00:07:25.699 --> 00:07:31.009
How can we get the calendars to be better,
hiring new people, we need more staff here,

00:07:31.009 --> 00:07:34.259
we need a person here, we need to fire this
person, we need to renegotiate this here,

00:07:34.259 --> 00:07:36.380
this thing's not working out, this vendor.

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:37.930
. . that's operations.

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:39.669
Operations are thinking like that.

00:07:39.669 --> 00:07:43.080
Operations will come in and say, I can make
the operations of this better.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:44.699
So critical.

00:07:44.699 --> 00:07:47.850
Maybe boring to some, it's so critical for
the company.

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:49.880
So that would be a COO.

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:51.240
Then we have finance.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:59.150
Finance is QuickBooks, payroll, commissions,
vendor checks, attorneys, making sure attorneys,

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:02.030
you're negotiating the contracts with the
attorneys the right way.

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:07.510
CPA, accounting, the team, assistant analyst,
all these things that you're going to have

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:09.630
to do when you're running finance.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:12.419
By the way, when you're a rookie entrepreneur,
guess what you are?

00:08:12.419 --> 00:08:14.630
Every one of them is what you are, okay?

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:16.259
So then you have the CFO on the finance side.

00:08:16.259 --> 00:08:17.780
Then you have expansion.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:19.290
Who's expansion?

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:23.590
Somebody that says, you know, we need to go
to a brand new marketplace.

00:08:23.590 --> 00:08:27.539
So expansion thinks about building a team
together, for us to go to a new market.

00:08:27.539 --> 00:08:32.839
This could be a Chief Expansion Officer -- there's
actually a Chief Expansion Officer -- it sounds

00:08:32.839 --> 00:08:35.659
like a CEO but it's a Chief Expansion Officer.

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:39.839
And they're job is simply, hey, we're not
on the East Coast, we want to go into the

00:08:39.839 --> 00:08:40.899
East Coast, what do we need to do?

00:08:40.899 --> 00:08:42.449
Bop, bop, bop, bop, bop.

00:08:42.449 --> 00:08:43.449
Great.

00:08:43.449 --> 00:08:46.209
Hey, we're not in South America, what do we
need to do?

00:08:46.209 --> 00:08:47.250
. . . . . For you, it may be local.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:50.999
Like somebody came and visited me when I was
in Palm Springs.

00:08:50.999 --> 00:08:55.720
He drove all the way down to come and visit
me and he runs a mobile detailing company.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:00.189
Which I'm very familiar with the mobile detailing
company, and nowadays it's getting to the

00:09:00.189 --> 00:09:03.170
app side, there's a lot of opportunity to
that as well, similar to Uber that you just

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:07.480
order something and somebody comes and services
your car, right?

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:12.079
And there's high end, $100, like right now,
literally three minutes before I did the video,

00:09:12.079 --> 00:09:14.290
my BMW i8, just got serviced.

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:18.220
Somebody comes, does it, you don't even see
it, it gets done, they pick it up, they clean

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:19.220
it, right?

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:20.220
Okay.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:21.649
So he came to me and he said, what do I do?

00:09:21.649 --> 00:09:27.499
So he's in San Diego, and how does he get
into maybe a Temecula market?

00:09:27.499 --> 00:09:28.829
How does he get into Riverside?

00:09:28.829 --> 00:09:30.379
How does he get into L.A.?

00:09:30.379 --> 00:09:34.040
The expansion for that person may be within
that area.

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:36.480
Somebody can put a strategy in how to do that.

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:37.480
Marketing.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:39.110
Marketing is just very, very critical.

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:40.309
This could be a CMO.

00:09:40.309 --> 00:09:44.079
Marketing could be when a customer thinks
about our company, what's the first word that

00:09:44.079 --> 00:09:45.209
comes to mind?

00:09:45.209 --> 00:09:48.351
What do our employees think about our company?

00:09:48.351 --> 00:09:51.509
What do our customers, what does the market,
what does our competitors, what does our enemy

00:09:51.509 --> 00:09:52.809
think about us?

00:09:52.809 --> 00:09:54.730
How do we want to market our product?

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:56.079
Which funnels are we using?

00:09:56.079 --> 00:09:57.300
Is it more about social media?

00:09:57.300 --> 00:09:58.300
Is it more about Internet?

00:09:58.300 --> 00:09:59.490
Should we go on T.V.?

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:01.439
What is our ROI on T.V.?

00:10:01.439 --> 00:10:06.009
Are we really doing this because we want people
to look at our commercial and come and buy?

00:10:06.009 --> 00:10:09.129
They don't really do it for that, but maybe
it's effective if we spend this 3.5 million

00:10:09.129 --> 00:10:13.550
dollars to put a commercial on Super Bowl
because our competitors are going to see that

00:10:13.550 --> 00:10:17.019
we're doing better or, you know, maybe our
existing customers are going to say, "Wow,

00:10:17.019 --> 00:10:20.720
I'm so glad that's my guy, that's my guy,"
so it keeps our clients with us and keeps

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:21.720
them proud.

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:22.999
Or no, we don't need to do Super Bowl.

00:10:22.999 --> 00:10:23.999
It's not our market.

00:10:23.999 --> 00:10:28.069
Our market watches this or, you know, our
demographic is people that are between 25

00:10:28.069 --> 00:10:30.189
- 35 years old, our guys watch T.V.

00:10:30.189 --> 00:10:31.189
Our guys don't watch soap operas.

00:10:31.189 --> 00:10:32.819
Why are we wasting our time doing.

00:10:32.819 --> 00:10:35.769
. . so marketing thinks that way.

00:10:35.769 --> 00:10:36.769
Okay?

00:10:36.769 --> 00:10:38.779
Complete different way.

00:10:38.779 --> 00:10:43.569
Marketing in a way also thinks about, a person
who's a marketer -- if you're a marketer,

00:10:43.569 --> 00:10:45.670
you know how you think.

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:47.709
Marketer's think completely different.

00:10:47.709 --> 00:10:52.040
They go into a restaurant and they literally
look at everything as a marketer.

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:54.050
It's a complete different eyeball that they
have.

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:55.110
Next is legal.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:56.989
What's legal?

00:10:56.989 --> 00:10:58.879
In every industry there's compliance.

00:10:58.879 --> 00:11:04.139
There's laws, there's you know, making sure
you are compliant with the regulators or whatever

00:11:04.139 --> 00:11:09.720
- with the financial industry it's FINRA,
it's SEC, you know, all these other things

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:11.019
that you're dealing with on that side.

00:11:11.019 --> 00:11:14.980
And real estate it's going to be Department
of Real Estate, the department of insurance

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:16.410
-- it doesn't matter what's you're doing.

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:20.930
Technology is going to be a whole different
law -- anything, there is legal.

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:24.260
So you've got to make sure you have a strong
legal team.

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:28.889
Sometimes the A-type personality, don't like
to touch this because it's -- we don't need

00:11:28.889 --> 00:11:30.699
any help with this.

00:11:30.699 --> 00:11:32.329
Why would we worry about compliance?

00:11:32.329 --> 00:11:33.329
C'mon, man.

00:11:33.329 --> 00:11:34.519
No one cares about compliance.

00:11:34.519 --> 00:11:35.519
Big mistake.

00:11:35.519 --> 00:11:36.519
Big mistake.

00:11:36.519 --> 00:11:38.779
Because you need the contracting to be right.

00:11:38.779 --> 00:11:44.209
. . I spend a lot of money in that area and
I have no problem with it because it's very,

00:11:44.209 --> 00:11:46.879
very critical to stay compliant with your
business.

00:11:46.879 --> 00:11:48.779
But you may be watching this and saying, that's
me.

00:11:48.779 --> 00:11:50.709
I love those types of things.

00:11:50.709 --> 00:11:51.709
Phenomenal.

00:11:51.709 --> 00:11:53.009
It could be information.

00:11:53.009 --> 00:11:54.009
What's information?

00:11:54.009 --> 00:11:56.820
Someone that's very good at research, someone
that's very good at studying competition,

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:01.959
somebody that's very good at studying trends
and keeping track of updating and saying,

00:12:01.959 --> 00:12:06.550
just so you know, today in the market . . . AIG
CEO was called out.

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:09.810
. . and this is what happened with him and
they said this, and did you hear about what

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:15.739
happened today in the Silicon Valley marketplace,
a new startup was raised 200 million dollars

00:12:15.739 --> 00:12:20.540
on their round three and their going after
this and did you hear about the Fintech market,

00:12:20.540 --> 00:12:21.639
whatever.

00:12:21.639 --> 00:12:23.889
Information guys are going to give you what
is going on today.

00:12:23.889 --> 00:12:27.812
And this is beneficial because this helps
the visionary know how to go to the next level,

00:12:27.812 --> 00:12:32.660
this helps the marketer know what adjustments
to make, this helps the expansion person know

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:36.660
what marketplaces we need to go and expand
and what not to waste our money and resources

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:37.660
into.

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:41.079
This helps finance know how to adjust, hey,
this is what's going on over the next six

00:12:41.079 --> 00:12:44.299
to 12 months, we should be experiencing this
or we should be experiencing that.

00:12:44.299 --> 00:12:45.679
That's information, so CIO.

00:12:45.679 --> 00:12:49.319
Then you have product development and product
development is what?

00:12:49.319 --> 00:12:53.100
Product development is somebody that says,
I'm designing a product.

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:56.230
Typically, product development are wired in
a completely different way.

00:12:56.230 --> 00:12:58.449
Completely different way.

00:12:58.449 --> 00:13:03.629
They will take a look at this packaging and
they'll say, you know, I wonder why they put

00:13:03.629 --> 00:13:04.980
these four things in the back?

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:06.629
I don't like it.

00:13:06.629 --> 00:13:12.230
I think it makes it flimsy and I think the
packaging should be done a little bit different,

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:16.800
see how hard it is, this should be easier
because when you do it this way, sometimes

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:22.699
it's tougher, so it takes three seconds to
package this with the machine, and.

00:13:22.699 --> 00:13:25.209
. . they just look at it different than you
and I do.

00:13:25.209 --> 00:13:31.329
A product development person could say, we
need to come out with a mortgage product today

00:13:31.329 --> 00:13:36.410
that is going to make the regulators happy
because the regulators are very concerned

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:41.739
about not having a neg am product that's a
payment or negative amortization, that's not

00:13:41.739 --> 00:13:46.389
going to effect this, so to come out with
this, but to make the cost low, why don't

00:13:46.389 --> 00:13:47.809
we get rid of this?

00:13:47.809 --> 00:13:48.809
Great.

00:13:48.809 --> 00:13:50.569
They work in a completely different way.

00:13:50.569 --> 00:13:54.379
Very important for product development to
be in communication with the rest of the guys.

00:13:54.379 --> 00:13:58.329
Regularly in the insurance agency I get calls
and I sit on several different boards, that

00:13:58.329 --> 00:14:01.759
they'll call me and they'll say, "What do
you think the insurance, financial industry

00:14:01.759 --> 00:14:03.839
needs to look at right now, next as a product?

00:14:03.839 --> 00:14:08.259
And we'll have a long conversation about areas
that I believe they need to completely change

00:14:08.259 --> 00:14:12.249
and revolutionize because the insurance industry
needs a lot of disruption right now.

00:14:12.249 --> 00:14:13.439
Then you have sales.

00:14:13.439 --> 00:14:15.389
By the way, nothing happens without sales.

00:14:15.389 --> 00:14:16.389
Just so you know this.

00:14:16.389 --> 00:14:24.209
So a lot of times, we put so much attention
into everything else, so much attention, every

00:14:24.209 --> 00:14:26.519
position is very important.

00:14:26.519 --> 00:14:28.609
Every position is very important.

00:14:28.609 --> 00:14:32.100
That you forget to put constant emphasis here
[sales].

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:37.079
If you look at companies that go through this
-- they start going through a downfall, you

00:14:37.079 --> 00:14:44.420
will see, almost every single time, if it
goes through this, 80% of the time, you can

00:14:44.420 --> 00:14:46.129
go and point it to sales.

00:14:46.129 --> 00:14:47.129
Why?

00:14:47.129 --> 00:14:50.359
A great sales manager is no longer there.

00:14:50.359 --> 00:14:56.569
A chairman of agency who was running the entire
sales department has gotten too casual because

00:14:56.569 --> 00:15:00.799
his bonuses are so easy now that he knows
he's going to hit the bonus within four months,

00:15:00.799 --> 00:15:02.063
and he's golfing four months out of the year.

00:15:02.063 --> 00:15:04.839
You've got to make some changes to the bonus
structure, right?

00:15:04.839 --> 00:15:11.009
So you have a person that is barely working
and you know nothing about it because everything

00:15:11.009 --> 00:15:15.149
is based on no schedule, so you think he's
working, but he's not and you need to make

00:15:15.149 --> 00:15:19.910
an adjustment and go and partner with Salesforce.com
because you need to make sure that the guys

00:15:19.910 --> 00:15:23.889
are logging in every single day, what appointment
they ran, and who was the person with and

00:15:23.889 --> 00:15:27.790
put a number there and you need to run a minimum
of this many appointments, you need to spend

00:15:27.790 --> 00:15:32.559
this much money, you cannot spend more than
this at a bar and . . . to track that.

00:15:32.559 --> 00:15:33.799
You can see this.

00:15:33.799 --> 00:15:37.889
You may be somebody watching this and saying,
I'm a salesperson, I'm a great sales trainer,

00:15:37.889 --> 00:15:39.429
I'm a great person that can build an army.

00:15:39.429 --> 00:15:40.429
And here's the weird thing.

00:15:40.429 --> 00:15:41.910
You want to know something?

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:45.279
They [sales] typically make more money than
every one of these guys.

00:15:45.279 --> 00:15:46.990
Just so you know this.

00:15:46.990 --> 00:15:47.990
Just so you know this.

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:49.579
These guys typically make the most money.

00:15:49.579 --> 00:15:51.040
I know this sounds strange.

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:53.759
They definitely make more money than these
guys [finance through product development].

00:15:53.759 --> 00:15:59.150
They make generally the most money, because
if these guys are not getting paid, what's

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:01.999
the motivation behind -- the company's done.

00:16:01.999 --> 00:16:05.290
You think Coca Cola survives without their
guys that are going out there selling Coca

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:08.149
Cola to the local -- no, it's a done deal.

00:16:08.149 --> 00:16:10.489
So sales is very, very critical.

00:16:10.489 --> 00:16:15.790
You've got to look at this and you may say,
"Pat, you know what, I'm very good with people.

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:20.279
I can pretty much go in a room and strike
up a conversation with anybody and get information

00:16:20.279 --> 00:16:25.139
and talk to people and they'll tell me anything
and I'm a chameleon, I can get along.

00:16:25.139 --> 00:16:26.759
. . well, you may be a biz dev person.

00:16:26.759 --> 00:16:27.889
Good for you.

00:16:27.889 --> 00:16:29.309
I'm a very strong sales person.

00:16:29.309 --> 00:16:30.309
I can sell.

00:16:30.309 --> 00:16:31.309
I'm good with sales.

00:16:31.309 --> 00:16:33.749
I know how to ask the right questions.

00:16:33.749 --> 00:16:34.799
I know that sales flow.

00:16:34.799 --> 00:16:36.040
I'm good at teaching this.

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:37.040
Excellent.

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:38.040
I'm a visionary.

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:39.040
I'm this.

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:40.040
I'm that.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:42.610
Regardless of what you are, you've got to
really take an assessment of yourself and

00:16:42.610 --> 00:16:49.959
ask, what is my strength, top three, what
is my dominate one that I am?

00:16:49.959 --> 00:16:51.429
For yourself.

00:16:51.429 --> 00:16:57.339
And what can I do to become better at that
by studying others who have done those areas.

00:16:57.339 --> 00:17:01.440
Now the complete opposite side, if you're
running a company, you've got to ask, who

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:03.449
is who?

00:17:03.449 --> 00:17:05.470
What am Iacking?

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:08.060
Generally as a startup, you're going to wear
seven different hats.

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:13.620
When you get started, you may be the visionary,
the biz dev, the sales, the operations, the

00:17:13.620 --> 00:17:16.180
finance, the expansion, all of these.

00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:21.850
And you may have an attorney that you put
on retainer that you have hired, but they're

00:17:21.850 --> 00:17:26.381
helping you and you give them $10,000 as a
retainer and everything else for you.

00:17:26.381 --> 00:17:28.920
You're Chief Information Officer is a guy
named Google.

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:30.390
You know, that's what you do.

00:17:30.390 --> 00:17:32.900
You're Googling everything to find out what
it is, right?

00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:35.100
So it doesn't matter what it is.

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:39.340
Eventually if you want to compete at the higher
market and if you want to get the bigger share

00:17:39.340 --> 00:17:44.670
of whatever industry you're part of, you need
all of these 10 different guys on your team

00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:49.040
and you need to know which role you play the
best and become the best you can within that

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:52.700
area, while knowing how all the other people
are wired as well.

00:17:52.700 --> 00:17:56.890
So hopefully today you may say, "Pat, so what
